    <string name="guide_modes_title">CAPTURE vs GLASS</string>
    <string name="guide_modes_body">CAPTURE copies the screen and runs the shader on the real image (colour, blur, curvature); Android asks you to share the screen each time, and apps with DRM (Netflix, Disney+) block it. GLASS draws a transparent layer on top with no capture: no permission, it works in protected apps and loses no frames, so it is also the performance mode. The price: it can only darken the image, so scanlines and masks work well and colour or blur effects do not.</string>
    <string name="guide_access_title">WHY ACCESSIBILITY</string>
    <string name="guide_access_body">ShaderVulkan uses an accessibility service for three things only: drawing the shader on top while letting your touches through, knowing which app is in front (for app profiles) and noticing when the notification shade opens, to pause. It does not read or store the content of your apps, and nothing is collected or sent anywhere.</string>
    <string name="guide_access_confirm">ShaderVulkan needs its accessibility service to draw the shader on top while letting your touches through, to know which app is in front (app profiles) and to notice the notification shade, to pause. It does not read or store the content of your apps, and nothing is collected or sent anywhere.\n\nNext you will go to Android\'s Accessibility settings: turn on ShaderVulkan Overlay Service.</string>
    <string name="guide_zone_title">ZONE AND BARS</string>
    <string name="guide_zone_body">The ZONE is the part of the screen where the shader applies; the rest stays untouched. Pick an aspect ratio, draw it by hand, or let AUTO detect the black bars of the content. BARS paints black over the sides outside the zone, and DUAL splits the screen into two zones (DS or 3DS style).</string>
    <string name="guide_combine_title">COMBINING SHADERS</string>
    <string name="guide_combine_body">COMBINE adds another shader to the active one. The list runs from top to bottom: ADD ABOVE puts a shader before (it processes the capture first) and ADD BELOW puts it after (it processes the previous result, for example a vignette over a CRT). Press APPLY to load the set; each load recompiles every pass.</string>
    <string name="guide_params_title">PARAMETERS AND FAVORITES</string>
    <string name="guide_params_body">Each shader has parameters you can change live from PARAMS or from SETTINGS. Save the current values as a PROFILE to get them back later. Mark the ones you use most with [ ] and turn on FAVORITES ONLY in the menu to hide the rest.</string>
    <string name="guide_fps_title">FPS COUNTER</string>
    <string name="guide_fps_body">UI is the refresh rate the screen is running at, IN the frames per second that arrive from the capture and OUT the ones the shader renders. IN and OUT alike is healthy; IN high and OUT low means the GPU or the shader cannot keep up; IN low means the source or the capture is the limit, not the shader. The counter turns red if the output stays well below the input.</string>
    <string name="guide_apps_title">APP PROFILES</string>
    <string name="guide_apps_body">An app profile remembers the shader, settings, zone and mode for one app. When you open it the shader turns on by itself (Glass at once, Capture asking to share the screen); when you leave it asks whether to pause or close. The profile ALL OTHER APPS applies to every app without its own.</string>
    <string name="guide_quick_title">QUICK START AND BATTERY</string>
    <string name="guide_quick_body">Add the Shader tile to the quick settings, or long-press the app icon, to turn the last shader on without opening the app. In SETTINGS, battery mode lowers the load below a battery level and the FPS cap limits the output to save power.</string>
    <string name="btn_fav_only">FAVORITES ONLY</string>
    <string name="menu_no_favorites">No favorites yet. Mark parameters with [ ] and they will show here.</string>

